description = "I set up Kubernetes to learn it, then packaged the second cluster so it would launch in an afternoon. Three short posts: what the kit costs and what it buys, where k3s is genuinely stronger than the simple options, and the one workload I know well enough to prove the edge of it."
+++

I set up Kubernetes to learn it. The cluster itself took an afternoon, but
everything after it took the rest of the week: delivery, secrets, certificates,
isolation between tenants, observability, backups. I did not want to pay for
that week twice, so the second cluster became a repository you can fork.

Three posts came out of it.

- **[K3s starter kit on Hetzner cloud](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/).** What it costs
  (**€122.45 a month**, measured), what it uses before any of your own code runs
  (**4,526 Mi**, also measured), and where k3s is genuinely stronger than the
  simple options. Which, under about ten services, is nowhere.

- **[What a game world server actually
  needs](/blog/what-a-game-world-server-needs/).** A WoW emulator is an old
  favourite of mine, and it is an unusually honest test of a cluster. Almost all
  of it is stateless plumbing that belongs in one. Exactly one process does not,
  and that is the one that is the game.

The platform itself is at
[hcloud-k3s-platform-kit](https://github.com/sasin91/hcloud-k3s-platform-kit):
fork it, edit the values, run it. Every decision has the reasoning attached, and
the forty-nine things that broke without saying anything are in a file of their
own.

If you have one application, none of this is for you. Put it on
[one machine](/blog/athletos-freebsd/) for about €7 a month and get on with it.
What flips the decision is rarely the number of machines. It is the second
person who has to keep the isolation correct, because that is where "we will
remember" stops being true.

Every number in these posts was measured on a cluster that existed. Where I
could not measure something, I say so instead of guessing.
